recipe.rubric:
Grade the words on a comparison page the way its reader at {{ account.name }} reads them: the headline, the intro, the two fit lines and the evidence lines. 10 is copy a buyer would trust from a vendor comparing itself with the tool the buyer already uses.
Score these, 0 to 10 each:
- honest: the intro says {{ data.current_tool }} is our read or our guess, not a fact, and matches the evidence (it says the site names the tool only when the evidence list has items). The fit line for {{ data.current_tool }} names only strengths from rows where better_fit is them; the fit line for us names only strengths from rows where better_fit is us. A strength that is not in the rows fails this check.
- grounded: every fact about {{ account.name }} comes from the site text or the notes below, and every evidence line points to something the site text says, with where it appears.
- plain: short sentences a person would say out loud. No marketing adjectives, no stacked nouns, nothing that talks {{ data.current_tool }} down.
- useful: the intro tells the reader why the page is worth a minute, and each fit line would help them decide.
Automatic fail: an em dash or en dash, an exclamation mark, a price, an email address or phone number, a feature or claim about {{ data.current_tool }} or about us that the rows below do not hold, or a fact about {{ account.name }} that the site text and notes below do not hold.
Rows, the only source for claims about either tool: {{ data.rows | json }}
Evidence the writer had: {{ data.evidence | json }}
Site text ({{ data.site.url | default: 'no site' }}): {{ data.site.title }} {{ data.site.description }} {{ data.site.markdown | truncate: 6000 }}
Notes: {{ account.dossier.summary | default: 'none' }} {{ account.dossier.why_now | default: '' }}
Pass when the overall score is 7.5 or higher and no check is 6 or lower. On a fail, name the check and quote the phrase to change. The fix never supplies replacement facts, numbers or claims.
